*added function /project_values/ in relational abstract domains
Relational, ApronWrapper, BoolPartition : implemented
BoolActivate : not implemented
Analyze : used
val project_values : t -> t -> (Ast.expr * Ast.Var.t) list -> t
project_values dom1 dom2 [(x+1, y); (z-1,k)] returns dom2 in which y = value of x+1 in dom1 and k = value of z-1 in dom1
Function project_values works like the function assignment but with a domain (dom1) to evaluate expressions (left elements of the relational list) and a domain (dom2) to assign variables (right elements of the relational list).
 
examples:

 m1 = [| x = [0;8], z = [2;2] |]  m2 = [| u = [0;0] |]
 Relational.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) [(x * z, u)] = Env(m3) with m3 = [| u = [0;16] |]

 m1 = [| x = [0;8], z = [2;2], p = top |]  m2 = [| u = top, g = top |] i.e. Env(m2) = top
 Relational.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) [(z - 2, u); (x, g)] = Env(m3) with m3 = [| u = [0;0], g = [0;8] |]

 Relational.project_values EnvBot dom1 l = EnvBot

 Relational.project_values dom1 EnvBot l = EnvBot

 Relational.project_values top dom1 l = dom1

 m1 = [| h = [-oo;6] |]  m2 = [| t = [1;1] |]
 Relational.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) [(i,o)] = Env(m2) because o is not contained in m2 so there are no values from Env(m1) to project in Env(m2)

 Relational.project_values and ApronWrapper.project_values should work almost in the same way.


 m1 = {bv1 = (true : dom1; false : dom2)}  m2 = {bv2 = (true : dom3; false : dom4)}
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) (bv1,bv2)::l = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv2 = ( true : dom5; false : dom6)}, dom5 = (project_values dom1 dom3 (bv1,bv2)::l) and dom6 = (project_values dom2 dom4 (bv1,bv2)::l)

 m1 = {bv1 = (true : dom1; false : dom2)}  m2 = {bv2 = (true : dom3; false : bottom)}
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) (bv1,bv2)::l = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv2 = ( true : dom4; false : dom5)}, dom4 = (project_values dom1 dom2 (bv1,bv2)::l) and dom5 = (project_values dom2 top (bv1,bv2)::l)

 m1 = {bv1 = (true : dom1; false : bottom)}  m2 = {bv2 = (true : dom2; false : top)} 
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env (m2) [(true, bv2)] = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv2 = (true : dom3; false: bottom)} and dom3 = (project_values top dom2 [(bv1,bv2)]) = dom2

 m1 = {bv1 = (true : dom1; false : bottom)}  m2 = {bv2 = (true : dom2; false : top)}
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env (m2) [(bv1, bv2)] = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv2 = (true : dom3; false: bottom)} and dom3 = (project_values dom1 dom2 [(bv1,bv2)]) = dom2

 m1 = { bv1 = ( true : dom2; false : dom3)}
 BoolPartition.project_values NoBool(dom1) Env(m1) l = Env(m2) with m2 = {bv1 = (true : dom4; false : dom5)}, dom4 = (project_values dom1 dom2 l) and dom5 = (project_values dom1 dom3 l)

 m1 = { bv1 = ( true : dom1; false : dom2), bv2 = ( true : dom3; false : dom4)}
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) NoBool(dom5) l = NoBool(dom6) with dom6 = (project_values dom7 dom5 l) and dom7 = (BoolPartition.gamma Env(m1))

 BoolPartition.project_values NoBool(dom1) NoBool(dom2) l = NoBool(dom3) with dom3 = (project_values dom1 dom2 l)

 m1 = { bv1 = ( true : dom1; false : dom2)}  m2 = { bv2 = (true : dom3; false : dom4), bv3 = (true : dom5; false : dom6)}  l = (bv1, bv3)::tl with bv2 not in tl 
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) l = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv2 = (true : dom7; false : dom8), bv3 = (true: dom9; false : dom10)}, dom7 = (project_values dom11 dom3 l), dom8 = (project_values dom11 dom4 l), dom9 = (project_values dom1 dom5 l), dom10 = (project_values dom2 dom6 l) and dom11 = (BoolPartition.gamma Env(m1))

 m1 = {bv1 = (true : dom1; false : dom2), bv2 = (true : dom3; false : dom4)}  m2 = {bv3 = (true : dom5; false : dom 6)}  l contains no boolean
 BoolPartition.project_values Env(m1) Env(m2) l = Env(m3) with m3 = {bv3 = ( true : dom7; false : dom8)}, dom7 = (project_values dom9 dom5 l), dom8 = (project_values dom9 dom6 l) and dom9 = (BoolPartition.gamma Env(m1))

In the case of BoolPartition, when the domain in which the values are projected already contains boolean which value should not change or contains no boolean, it’s necessary to still project into partitions non boolean values of expressions in the relation on variables of the relation. But, which values to project? The choice made was to join values of the expression in every partitions of the first domain with the function gamma, and then project this value to every partition concerned.



*added constructor /Nde/ for type stm
type stm = … | Nde of Location.t * Name.t list * Name.t * expr list
Ast.fprint_stm, Ast.loc_of_stm, Ast.vars_of_stm, Ast.get_main_while_loc, Nn.process_ast : updated
PrintResults.print, PrintResults.print_invariants, Analyze.analyze : updating body functions and signature functions
Nde(l, [x;y;z], node1, [a;b;c]) => (x,y,z) = node1(a,b,c);
The constructor Nde works like statement Asn(_,_,Call(_)) but it allow the assignment of multiple variable at the same time.
The only way to construct a statement Nde is to translate a lustre code to tiny using lustrev. It can not be construct following parse of a tiny code.
In a lustre program, a node call we be translated with lustrev, into a statement Nde in Tiny.
It is not typed because variables assigned have different types, which is impossible to represents in tiny, but lustrev compiler should have already check the correct typing.



